- A double click on a file for a main already running adds that file to the input files of the running main, and signals the running main to wake up, for example bringing the window of the running main to the front.
- A 'captured extension' that no main claims gets an icon of its own, showing that the manager holds the extension, instead of the conflict icon.
- TODO: an error written in the Output of a project may need to offer an action that fixes the cause of that error, as the manager asks the user to let the manager hold an extension.
- A main learns the extensions the main claims, for example through a `System` method.

## Implementation notes

- The program folder of a manager is `fearlessManaged<version>`, `fearlessManaged<version>.app` on macOS; its two 'launchers' are `fearlessManaged<version>` and `fearlessManaged<version>w`, the identities of the manager in the Fearless family; managerFolder(l) is the folder `fearless<version>` next to the program folder of l.
- The name of the manager, the `name` of `Reconciliator.reconcile`, is `Fearless Manager`.
- The lock of the 'manager folder': the lock (`FileChannel.tryLock`) of the file `instance.lock` in the 'manager folder'. The operating system releases the lock when the process ends, however the process ends.
  The process holding the lock writes the identifier of its OS process, and the port of its connections, at the start of `instance.lock`, and locks only bytes of `instance.lock` after the identifier and the port: a manager process connects to that port, and `replaced` ends the process with that identifier. On Windows a process can not read the bytes that another process has locked.
- The connection of a message: TCP on the loopback address, on the port that the desk picks for the process holding the lock; a message, its Origin, and the Answer are UTF-8 text.
- The manager program is a Java program, so every message starts a JVM, and a message takes at least the time a JVM takes to start: that time is accepted, not a bug.
- `manager FearlessCommandServe` starts in a process of its own, independent of the process that runs it: the end of that process, or of its terminal, does not end the 'single' manager.
- The bytes of a `Base16:` path: on Windows the UTF-16 code units of the name, little endian (a name may contain an unpaired surrogate); elsewhere the bytes of the name as the file system stores them.
- Each main and each compilation runs in a JVM of its own (`Coordinator.run`, see CoordinatorDesign).
- A main or a compilation that takes all the memory ends alone, not the manager: on Linux with systemd as init (`/run/systemd/system` is a folder), `Fs.processBuilder` starts each child with `systemd-run --user --scope`, in a cgroup of its own.
  A cgroup is a group of processes the Linux kernel accounts together; systemd-oomd ends a whole cgroup, and a child process starts in the cgroup of the parent process.
- A main or a compilation ending with the manager: the manager starts the JVM of each main and of each compilation with a 'parent lifeline' (see Processes in AttenuationDesign) on a pipe the manager holds open.
- `replaced` deleting a 'manager folder': holding the lock of that folder, `replaced` renames the folder to a new name next to it, then deletes the renamed folder. When the rename fails, `replaced` deletes every file of the folder that can be deleted. On Windows a folder containing a file that a process has open may not be renamed.
- Windows always has a tray: the notification area of the taskbar.
- The 'tray icon window' is an AWT window of type popup.
  A Wayland client can not place its windows; AWT on Linux is an X11 client, through XWayland under Wayland.
  On X11 no taskbar lists a popup window, and a popup window stays where the manager places the popup window.
- Tray on Linux: `java.awt.SystemTray` needs an XEmbed tray; GNOME and most Wayland desks have none.
  When the session bus has an owner of the name `org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher`, the tray icon is a StatusNotifierItem registered with that watcher, with the menu served as `com.canonical.dbusmenu`.
  The manager speaks the D-Bus wire protocol itself, on the `unix:path=` socket of `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`.
  Otherwise, when `java.awt.SystemTray` is supported, the manager uses `java.awt.SystemTray`, as on Windows.
  A desk with neither has no tray.
- Clicks on the tray icon on Linux: some desks show the menu served as `com.canonical.dbusmenu` themselves.
  The other desks call `Activate`, `SecondaryActivate` or `ContextMenu` of the item, with the point of the click; the manager answers each of the three by opening the menu of the tray icon at that point.
- On Windows the manager copies into the folder icons of the 'manager folder' also the `.ico` made from the PNG of the conflict icon and of the Fearless icon, and a running main gives its windows the `.ico` of `Coordinator.iconFiles` directly.
- The exact rules and errors of the claims are those of `inject.Methods.checkClaims` of Frontend and of the doc comments of `OpenWith`, `Shortcut` and `PreLoadIcon` in `base`.
- GNOME types every empty file as `application/x-zerosize`, whatever the name of the file, and never hands an empty file to the manager.
- Renaming the first marker moves the marker to a file `<random UUID>.tmp` of the project folder, then to `<name>.fearless`.
- The last premise of `ok(i, n)` in name inference: on a file system ignoring case, that other file and the file `n.fearless` are one file.
